Key Features

* Natural-language appointment requests
* AI intent classification
* Structured JSON extraction
* Dynamic date and time extraction
* Relative-date interpretation
* Google Calendar availability checking
* Double-booking prevention
* One-hour appointment scheduling
* Appointment creation
* Appointment rescheduling
* Appointment cancellation
* Google Calendar Event ID tracking
* Missing-information validation
* Telegram confirmations and notifications
* Separation of scheduling and general inquiries

⸻

Error Prevention

Several safeguards are included in the workflow:

Missing scheduling information

Calendar operations are not executed when the required date or time is unavailable.

Double bookings

The requested time range is checked before a new Calendar event is created.

AI hallucination

The AI is instructed not to invent missing dates, times, customer information, appointment IDs, or Calendar availability.

Calendar Event IDs

Google Calendar generates the Event ID. The AI does not generate or guess Event IDs.

General inquiries

Questions about pricing, services, and payment are prevented from unnecessarily triggering Calendar operations.

⸻

What I Learned

This project helped me practice:

* Designing multi-branch n8n workflows
* Integrating AI with deterministic automation logic
* Using structured OpenAI outputs
* Working with Google Calendar
* Checking time-slot availability
* Handling date/time and timezone data
* Preventing scheduling conflicts
* Validating AI-generated data before API operations
* Managing appointment state
* Using external IDs to update existing records
* Separating AI reasoning from business logic

One of the main lessons from this project was that AI should interpret the customer’s request, while deterministic workflow logic should control actions such as availability checks, event creation, cancellation, and record updates.

